{
    "word": "unhyphenated",
    "lang_code": "en",
    "source_file_to_entries": {
        "raw-wiktextract-data.jsonl": [
            {
                "entry_id": "c55cd050-30c5-3e75-a491-9d9e00e42df7",
                "word": "unhyphenated",
                "pos": "adj",
                "lang_code": "en",
                "forms": [],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "f93b4bc7-4e85-32d9-b527-85198de013d4",
                        "entry_id": "c55cd050-30c5-3e75-a491-9d9e00e42df7",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Lacking a hyphen."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "not-comparable"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "f7d2a6f6-5980-4c8a-bb02-c1f4cf187722",
                                "sense_id": "f93b4bc7-4e85-32d9-b527-85198de013d4",
                                "text": "The word cooperation is unhyphenated, though some also spell it as co-operation."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "4086af15-2cea-38f1-8823-508257632223",
                        "entry_id": "c55cd050-30c5-3e75-a491-9d9e00e42df7",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Of people, belonging to a single ethnicity or nationality."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "Canada",
                            "US",
                            "not-comparable"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "724de694-a055-4008-9894-7c8f0b7c1b02",
                                "sense_id": "4086af15-2cea-38f1-8823-508257632223",
                                "text": "... when we shall recognize in our press and at the ballot box no votes but American votes, and no citizens but simply unhyphenated American citizens; when we shall insist that every man who places a ballot in our ballot box shall be able to read that ballot in the language of Lincoln and Grant, we shall have reached the hour when we may begin the most momentous and delicate task ever set before a people—the creation of a great nation out of the fragments of many diverse and even hostile nationalities."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "f6a5e09f-f99e-4a1c-9618-7e45f0f09a8a",
                                "sense_id": "4086af15-2cea-38f1-8823-508257632223",
                                "text": "For Pennsylvania yields to no State in the courage and virility of her sons—plain, unhyphenated Americans, not yet pushed by invaders off the soil; loyal men, whose fathers gave their blood like water for the Union."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "a7a68a96-8d2f-4c0b-b066-afd0285551e7",
                                "sense_id": "4086af15-2cea-38f1-8823-508257632223",
                                "text": "First of all, and probably greatest of all in view of most present-day writers, they were \"unhyphenated!\" This is indeed wonderful! And again, most wonderful! Unhyphenated! So interesting a fact as this, and one so eminently desirable, invites most critical investigation, for the very breath of suggestion insinuates at once that \"hyphenism\" and \"Americanism\" are by the very nature of things contradictory, and that \"hyphenism\" must needs be on a par with, if not exactly the same as, treason. We are therefore very much interested in the new fact that Pennsylvania's glory lies in the giving of \"unhyphenated\" Americans to the new nation born of the travails of the Revolution."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "b235370e-341b-4a34-a5b2-8836692cce1e",
                                "sense_id": "4086af15-2cea-38f1-8823-508257632223",
                                "text": "Some believe it is an expression of Hebrew-Americanism, others think it is an expressio of Negro-Americanism, still other's think it is pure American, unhyphenated."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "6d460f2c-e288-411d-9ef5-0d5813ee9a3c",
                                "sense_id": "4086af15-2cea-38f1-8823-508257632223",
                                "text": "The concept that Canada was \"one nation\" and its citizens \"unhyphenated\", in Diefenbaker's words, had to be set aside."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "de713aa7-4989-45b1-8941-8d6bb42e5135",
                                "sense_id": "4086af15-2cea-38f1-8823-508257632223",
                                "text": "The score for Unhyphenated white men, 36.0, falls just about midway between all white men and black men; the mean prestige score for Unhyphenated white women is 33.6, is considerably closer to the level for black women than all white women."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "b35fcfdf-c21e-462f-86e4-c2fbc1daaa18",
                                "sense_id": "4086af15-2cea-38f1-8823-508257632223",
                                "text": "Early on Trudeau scorned the idea of making everyone \"good, clean, unhyphenated Canadians,\" and the Reform is explicitly opposed to the Trudeau government's bilingual and multicultural programs."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "c312fa53-6490-4ab0-b67c-5d9a6cf1ae47",
                                "sense_id": "4086af15-2cea-38f1-8823-508257632223",
                                "text": "Stanley Lieberson (1985) has used the term unhyphenated white to identify a growing group of Americans who lack any clear-cut identification with or knowledge of specific European origins."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "2dbf2fb1-3e10-4150-9f7a-a5398212fe31",
                                "sense_id": "4086af15-2cea-38f1-8823-508257632223",
                                "text": "Who—what—were African Americans becoming in relation to political change in America and Africa? Would they simply become unhyphenated Americans, or, in gaining formal equality, would they enact a transnational American citizenship in solidarity with African peoples and in so doing participate in the democratization of America?"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "03995f45-3c08-454a-9238-f8210160820a",
                                "sense_id": "4086af15-2cea-38f1-8823-508257632223",
                                "text": "2007, Herb Duerr, The Unhyphenated Canuck: Reflections and Confessions of an Opinionated Immigrant, iUniverse.com\nThe Unhyphenated Canuck: Reflections and Confessions of an Opinionated Immigrant (title)"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "4ae7613f-ba7d-4838-bc7e-efbb9464bd6d",
                                "sense_id": "4086af15-2cea-38f1-8823-508257632223",
                                "text": "The color barriers in superhero comic books began to fall in 1969 when Marvel Comics introduced the Falcon, the first African-American superhero (the Black Panther, an unhyphenated African superhero preceded him three years prior) in Captain America #117."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
